    'verified',
    'Jenny Jump takes its name from a legend whose variant versions reflect generations of storytelling: a child fleeing in panic from an encounter in the woods, her father''s voice urging her to jump from a cliff. The legend is now understood by historians to encode colonial-era anxieties about Native peoples, and some versions have been revised in response. The forest itself — rocky ridges, dense canopy, and valley overlooks — creates what visitors consistently describe as two registers at once: the straightforwardly beautiful and the subtly unsettling, an atmosphere many describe as charged.',
    ARRAY['https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1504052434569-70add5ae4832?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1400&q=80', 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1518173946689-a252907891f2?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1400&q=80']::TEXT[],
