    'The 51-room Ringwood Manor has been the focal point of iron-making history in New Jersey since the colonial era, serving as home to several prominent families and reportedly never quite letting go of them. Visitors to the manor describe a sense of eyes on them in the upper rooms, and the surrounding 4,000-acre state park — deeply wooded, quiet, and largely unmarked by modernity — creates an atmosphere many describe as both restorative and uncanny. Historical records note that ironworkers from the 18th and 19th centuries are buried in unmarked graves throughout the property.',
